<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  mkskill config prototype (new era). One XML per project tree: the main
  <project> carries every <child-project> nested inside — children have no
  config file of their own.

  Attribute notes:
  - id: unique within the whole tree; the prefix for cross-unit references.
    Optional — a missing id gets an automatic "P0", "P1", … (with a warning;
    explicit ids are recommended as soon as anything references the unit).
    "P0" is reserved for the main project.
  - name: optional — defaults to the folder name (the base for the main
    project, the path folder for a child).
  - project-type: optional — defaults to "Other".
  - path (child only): mandatory, relative to the parent's root.
  - embed-parent (on a child's <embed>): which of the parent's generated
    views this child embeds too — "*" for all, or a fine-grained list.
  - fm-gen: how imported sources get their frontmatter — "embed" (default,
    injected at the top of the copy) or "extern" (a sibling .fm file);
    ",preserve" keeps an existing .fm as is instead of regenerating it.
  - <scan> is reserved: debug output of Save with IncludeScanData; readers
    recognize it and skip it entirely.

  Macros in this config — {$$$ ref $$$}:
  Expanded when the XML is parsed — EARLY, one lazy load pass closed over
  this one file. Markdown keeps its own <$$$msk.…$$$>, resolved LATE at
  composition time with its softer universe. Two skins, two moments; each
  parser knows one syntax and the foreign form stays literal — a visible
  hole beats a silent one. Legal in any
  attribute value and in the <entry>/<create> text nodes, whole document,
  under five laws that keep it sane:
    1. The universe's own SOURCES never expand: id, name, path,
       project-type — the parser builds the tree and the reference space
       from them (name feeds prj:id:name; expanding it would be circular).
       Braces there: LOAD ERROR, never a silent literal — the user typed
       a macro, silence would confuse. Every OTHER claimed attribute and
       text value is an Expandable: the raw stays in the document (a
       rewrite never freezes a template), the render feeds the consumers,
       and the user decides where a macro makes sense.
    2. Two phases: the whole tree parses literal first; references resolve
       after, against the complete tree. Document order never matters.
    3. Expansion produces values, never syntax: results are not re-scanned
       for macros and cannot inject structure. Composition happens in the
       label graph, guarded by its cycle rule.
    4. An unresolvable reference is a LOAD ERROR (composed docs keep
       their softer warn-and-leave). EVERY label of every domain resolves
       at load, cited or not — so the Expandables always resolve on firm
       ground, a broken or cyclic label fails EVERY verb, and the orphan
       is nobody's blind spot. Renders are not memoized: the mutators
       edit the in-memory state and read again. -vcheck stays the guard
       for what load cannot see: destinations, anchors, drift.
    5. In attributes, bare references only — no verbs, no connectors;
       literal text around them is fine (path assembly). Every
       composition or format is a computed label: want another render,
       declare another label.

  Reference grammar — the first segment picks the domain:
    name           local domain. In version-spec: components, version, ts.
                   In a project: name, id, type, base.
    label:key      a label of the LOCAL domain (value or computed alike).
    prj:id:var     a unit's structural variable from anywhere: base, name
                   (the necessary set; more by the same grammar when a
                   real case asks).
    prj:id:label:key   that unit's label.
    ver:ref        the version domain from project land: ver:version,
                   ver:ts, ver:major…, ver:label:key.
    glb:label:key  a global label.
  prj: always carries its id — a bare "prj:base" is a malformed
  reference; the local form is the bare name, the addressed form is
  complete, and no middle form exists.
  Labels are the ONE named-value system of this universe. A unit's <meta>
  keys stay a docs affair (their precedence chain, their world) — the
  config does not read them.

  Labels live in more places than the version-spec, and POSITION DEFINES
  THE DOMAIN: a <label> child of <mkskill> is global (glb:), inside a
  <project> it belongs to that unit (prj:id:label:), inside <version-spec>
  it is a version label (label: locally, ver:label: from outside). Same
  element, same two classes (value / computed), same one cycle DFS across
  every domain. volatile and default-to-ts are version-spec ONLY — they
  are bump semantics; elsewhere a label is plain value or computed. All
  the resolution cost sits in the load pass; after it, every consumer is
  a dictionary lookup.

  Variables are LOOKUPS on the parsed tree — anything that needs cooking
  is a computed label you declare. The catalog:
    structural     id, name, type, base — base is the RESOLVED unit root
                   (parent base + path; the tree root for main), computed
                   in the skeleton phase. path stays declared, literal,
                   and is not a variable.
    version        components by name, version (canonical render), ts
    labels         label:key per domain (glb / prj:id / ver)
  Doc-only variables (<meta> keys, view, meta.X, section front matter,
  badge, install, skill.usage.*) do not exist in the config universe.
  Bare names cannot collide: components live in ver, structural in prj —
  different domains by construction.
  The universe is CLOSED over this XML: nothing citable lives outside it —
  no sources, no markdown, no git, no filesystem except the destinations
  at write time. The v-verbs are fast by construction, not by
  optimization: parse one file, resolve what is reachable, touch the
  destinations, exit.
-->
<mkskill>

  <!-- global labels: written once, pointed at from anywhere (glb:label:copyright) -->
  <label key="copyright">Pablo Botella Navarro</label>

  <!--
    <version-spec> — the tree's version model. One number for the whole tree
    (main + children). This XML is the source of truth: the number exists
    here BEFORE any tag does; git only consumes it. mkskill produces output —
    it never tags, never publishes, never touches git.

    The command family — two mutate, one repairs, one guards, one reads:
      mkskill -vinc-<component>   increment a declared component, reset
                                  everything to its right, propagate — one
                                  atomic gesture: numbers, ts, labels,
                                  history entry, every update/create.
      mkskill -vset "0.7.4"       set outright (dot-separated, full arity,
                                  positional over params) — same gesture.
      ... -label:<key> "text"     set a label inline; rides on either
                                  mutator, never alone.
      mkskill -vbuild             re-propagate from the current XML state —
                                  no number, no ts, no history. Idempotent
                                  repair of drifted destinations.
      mkskill -vcheck             verify without writing: every destination
                                  matches today's render, replace anchors
                                  exist, needed labels are set, .gitignore
                                  holds what git-ignore promised. The CI
                                  leg — CI never bumps. Exhaustive: it
                                  alone resolves the whole spec,
                                  referenced or not — the guard sees what
                                  the fast verbs skip.
      mkskill -vout <ref>         print one render, bare reference only
                                  (version, ts, a component, label:key) —
                                  formatting lives in the XML, declare a
                                  computed label and print it by name.
                                  -tag is the daily alias of -vout version
                                  (git tag (mkskill -tag) in the bat).
    Without a mutation nothing propagates; without a version-spec every
    v-command errors. Everything validates BEFORE anything is written:
    unknown component, arity or domain mismatch, locked component — hard
    error. No carry, no saturation: overflow is an error. Updates and
    creates run in declaration order.
    Misplaced spec: <version-spec> inside a <project> is a WARNING and the
    element is ignored (its meaning there stays reserved).

    Macro grammar in the entry/create TEXT nodes (attributes: bare refs
    only, law 5):
      {$$$ name $$$}                a value by reference
      {$$$ %02d name $$$}           optional Go fmt verb before the name —
                                    %d, %02d, %x, %X… fmt verbatim
      {$$$ 'literal' + value $$$}   connector + any of the above; empty
                                    value: the piece vanishes, connector
                                    included. That is the WHOLE grammar:
                                    % opens a verb, a quote opens a
                                    connector, anything else is a name.
    Reserved names: version (the canonical render), ts.

    - format: optional override of the canonical render — a Go fmt string
      applied positionally to the components in params order
      (format="v%d.%d.%d"). Same verbs as the macro holes. Absent, the
      render is "v" + the components joined by dots (v0.5.0).
      {$$$ version $$$} and -tag print it either way.
    - params: ordered components with domains — name:byte, name:word,
      name:[lo-hi]. Order is significance: incrementing one resets every
      component to its right to its minimum.
    - lock: a locked component refuses -vinc and -vset alike; the conscious
      gesture to move it is editing this file.
    - <ts>: UTC RFC-3339 (Z), stamped on every mutation. Empty = never
      bumped. Timezones deliberately deferred: everything is UTC.
    - <label>: two exclusive classes, one consumption (label:key).
      VALUE label — the text node IS the current value, and the universal
      rule reaches it: the text may carry {$$$ $$$} macros — the document
      keeps the raw, consumers get the render, and cycles through value
      texts are caught by the same guard. A mutation without -label:<key>
      resets it: default-to-ts="true" materializes the new ts,
      default="literal" writes that literal. The two defaults are
      mutually exclusive. The history archives value labels RENDERED — a
      log freezes what was true, that is its job.
      COMPUTED label — format + params (positional: %d components, %s
      labels), never stored, always derived. when="ref" guards it: empty
      referenced value, empty label — and the emptiness propagates to
      connectors downstream. May reference components and other labels;
      cycles are a load error. volatile, defaults and -label:<key> on a
      computed label: load error.
      Another composition, another format? Another label — they are cheap
      on purpose; consumers only point.
    - <update file type="json|xml|replace">: patch a file that other
      content also owns; file is project-root-relative; replace is the
      default — the only type that assumes nothing about the file. An
      <entry>'s text node is always the rendered value; the address
      differs by type:
        json     key="ruta/ruta/key" — slash path; flat file = one segment.
        xml      same key path down to the element; attrib="name" targets
                 an attribute (absent = the element's text content).
                 attrib is xml-only — on any other type it is a load
                 error. Patched through cargoxml: foreign content
                 survives untouched.
        replace  no address — each line self-anchors: the static skeleton
                 (the text outside the macros) locates the file line, and
                 the whole line is rewritten with the render. A multi-line
                 entry anchors line by line. Zero matching lines: error.
                 Several: all rewritten — coordinating the number
                 everywhere is the point.
      Entry whitespace: trim="all|left|right|none" (default all) strips
      each template line; matching compares trimmed against trimmed.
      indent: output indentation in spaces — absent, replace keeps the
      indentation the file line already had; explicit forces it.
      EOL: update preserves the file's newline style.
    - <create>: a file wholly owned by the version — written entire, no
      anchors. The template is the text node, or src="path" to an external
      one — mutually exclusive, both at once is a load error; same macros
      in either. The newline right after the opening tag is not part of
      the output. eol="lf|crlf" declares the output's newlines — absent,
      the template goes as it arrives: inline is LF by XML law (§2.11
      normalizes line breaks on input), src is verbatim. A .bat wants
      eol="crlf". git-ignore="true" appends the file to .gitignore if
      missing (derived output is never committed). overwrite: "true"
      (default) regenerates every propagation; "warn" leaves an existing
      file and says so; "false" leaves it and fails — scaffold-once
      semantics.
    - embed-version (on a unit's <embed>): a template attribute, not a
      boolean — its expanded value is what the generated embed carries and
      what the CLI's version command prints. Absent: no version in the
      embed. One bare reference, per law 5 — compose in a label.

    Around it, not mkskill's business: git. The bat tags with the -tag
    output; CI never bumps — it runs -vcheck and compares the pushed tag
    against -tag, failing when this file and reality disagree.
  -->
  <version-spec format="v%d.%d.%d" params="major:byte,minor:[0-99],build:word">
    <version major="0" minor="5" build="0" lock="major">
      <ts></ts>
    </version>
    <label key="title" volatile="true" default-to-ts="true">version description</label>
    <label key="vlong" format="version: %d.%d build: %03.3d" params="major,minor,build"></label>
    <label key="desc" format="%s - version %d.%d build: %03.3d" params="label:title,major,minor,build" when="label:title"></label>

    <update file="/.build/next-release.json" type="json">
      <entry key="tag">{$$$ version $$$}</entry>
      <entry key="title">{$$$ label:desc $$$}</entry>
    </update>
    <update file="path-to-a-c-header.h">
      <entry trim="all|left|right|none" indent="0">
        #define APP_VERSION "{$$$ version $$$}"
        #define APP_VERSION_L "{$$$ label:vlong $$$}"
      </entry>
    </update>
    <create file="__publish.bat" git-ignore="true" overwrite="true">
git tag {$$$ version $$$}
git push origin main --tags
</create>
  </version-spec>

  <project id="main" name="project-name" project-type="Go-Module|Go-CLI|Go|JS-Bundle|Other">
    <embed filename="path-to-embed-file-relative-to-base-project" module-name="module-name"   embed-version="{$$$ ver:label:vlong $$$}"/>
    <import-miniskin content="relative-path-to-content-base" fm-gen="embed|extern|extern,preserve"/>
    <preserve>
      <item file="./README.md"/>
    </preserve>
    <child-project id="child-id" name="child-project-name" path="relative-path-to-child-project-root" project-type="Go-CLI">
      <embed filename="path-to-embed-file-relative-to-base-project" module-name="module-name" embed-parent="*|readme,agents,skill"/>
      <import-miniskin content="relative-path-to-content-base" fm-gen="embed|extern|extern,preserve"/>
      <preserve>
        <item file="./README.md" method="alt|alias" alias="child-readme.md"/>
      </preserve>
    </child-project>
  </project>

  <!--
    <version-history> — opt-in mutation log, child of <mkskill>, NOT of
    <version-spec>. You decide IF (by placing this tag) and WHERE (by its
    position; the end keeps the editable zone compact — growth never pushes
    your working area). The tool only ever inserts entries AT THE TOP —
    newest first, so the last release is always the first line — and never
    creates the element itself: no tag, no history. It records number
    mutations, not releases: every -vinc/-vset inserts the state it
    produced, with method="vinc-<component>|vset" saying which. VALUE
    labels are archived by key (title="…"); computed labels are not —
    they are derivable. max="N" caps it FIFO, trimmed at the bottom where
    the old entries live. All UTC.
  -->
  <version-history max="10">
    <release version="v0.5.0" ts="2026-07-31T21:41:02Z" title="Patata Frita" method="vinc-build"/>
  </version-history>

</mkskill>
